  "worked_solution": "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(-3,\\,1).$  Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{\u2032},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x-6y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ -6y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-2x}{-6y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(-3,\\,1)$ is \n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(-3,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{x}{3y})}_{(-3,\\,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is ${m}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{-1}=1.$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $(-3,\\,1)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & ={m}^{\u2032}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-1) & =1(x+3) \\\\ y & =(x+3)+1 \\\\ y & =x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $x$-axis. At the point $P$ the $y$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $y=0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+4 \\\\ 0 & =x+4 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(-4,\\,0).$",
